# SPOT Qubes OS RPC policy
#
# This file documents the intended minimum RPC boundary.
# It is deliberately restrictive.
#
# SPOT should NOT receive unrestricted dom0 access.
#
# Exact syntax may vary with the Qubes OS version and RPC
# service definitions installed on the system. This file is
# therefore treated as a policy specification/template and
# should be reviewed before installation.
#
# Security goals:
#
#   1. Default deny.
#   2. No direct dom0 administration.
#   3. No unrestricted VM creation/removal.
#   4. No unrestricted device attachment.
#   5. No credential access.
#   6. No arbitrary file access.
#   7. Permit only narrowly scoped SPOT services.
#
# Intended SPOT service classes:
#
#   spot.SessionInfo
#   spot.NetworkStatus
#   spot.PersonaStatus
#   spot.DisposableStatus
#
# These services should expose status and narrowly scoped
# operations rather than arbitrary Qubes administration.
#
# Example policy concept:
#
#   service: spot.SessionInfo
#       allow: spot-client
#
#   service: spot.NetworkStatus
#       allow: spot-client
#
#   service: spot.PersonaStatus
#       allow: spot-client
#
#   service: spot.DisposableStatus
#       allow: spot-client
#
# Everything else:
#
#   deny
#
# Particularly sensitive Qubes administration operations should
# remain unavailable to the normal SPOT process:
#
#   admin.vm.Start
#   admin.vm.Kill
#   admin.vm.Remove
#   admin.vm.device.Attach
#   admin.vm.device.Detach
#   admin.vm.volume.Import
#   admin.vm.volume.Resize
#   admin.vm.property.Set
#
# If a future SPOT feature requires one of these operations,
# create a dedicated narrowly scoped RPC service instead of
# granting the generic administrative service.
#
# Example:
#
#   BAD:
#       SPOT -> unrestricted admin.vm.*
#
#   GOOD:
#       SPOT -> spot.CreateDisposable
#             -> restricted helper
#             -> one explicitly validated Qube operation
#
# The restricted helper should validate:
#
#   - persona identity
#   - Qube name
#   - template
#   - network assignment
#   - resource limits
#   - disposable status
#   - emergency-stop state
#
# It should reject:
#
#   - dom0 targets
#   - arbitrary shell commands
#   - arbitrary RPC forwarding
#   - arbitrary device attachment
#   - arbitrary file access
#   - credential operations
#   - policy modification
